# Code Style

## Imports

- Always use the `@/*` path alias for cross-folder imports.
- Order: `react`/`next` → third-party → `@/...` → relative.
- No default-export-only modules unless required by Next.js (pages, layouts, route handlers).

```ts
// ✅
import { useEffect } from "react";
import { motion } from "motion/react";
import { cn } from "@/lib/utils";
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";
```

## Naming

- Components: `PascalCase` (file + export).
- Hooks: `useThing.ts` returning camelCase API.
- Constants: `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` and live under `src/constants/` or `src/data/`.
- Types/Interfaces: `PascalCase`, prefer `type` aliases over `interface` unless declaration merging is needed.

## Components

- Functional components only. Keep them small; extract sub-components when they exceed ~150 lines.
- Server-by-default, client only when needed (see `nextjs-app-router` rule).
- Do **not** add comments that just narrate the code (`// import x`, `// return jsx`). Comments should explain non-obvious intent only.

## Utilities

- `cn()` from `@/lib/utils` is the **only** way to compose conditional Tailwind classes.
- Reusable hooks live in `src/hooks/`, not inline.

## Type safety

- No `any` in app code (`src/app/`, `src/components/sections/`, `src/components/*.tsx`).
- `any` is tolerated inside `src/components/ui/*` (vendored shadcn) only.
- Don't use `// @ts-ignore` — use `// @ts-expect-error` with a one-line explanation when truly necessary.
